{"parentUuid":"9d58f08b-af7a-42d1-83b0-ddd4334093e4","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/workspace/agent/payme_deck/opening_mockups.html","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/agent/payme_deck/opening_mockups.html","content":"<!doctype html><html lang=\"he\" dir=\"rtl\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width,initial-scale=1\">\n<link rel=\"preconnect\" href=\"https://fonts.googleapis.com\">\n<link href=\"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Heebo:wght@400;700;800;900&display=swap\" rel=\"stylesheet\">\n<style>\n:root{\n  --bg:#171B3A; --card:#242B54; --card-hi:#2E3667; --brand:#2A315D;\n  --accent:#2FC2BC; --accent-d:#22A9A3;\n  --border:rgba(255,255,255,0.09); --border-acc:rgba(47,194,188,0.36);\n  --text:#F4F5FB; --gray:#A7ADCC; --dim:rgba(255,255,255,0.3);\n}\n*,*::before,*::after{box-sizing:border-box;margin:0;padding:0}\nbody{background:#0d1030;font-family:'Heebo',sans-serif;-webkit-font-smoothing:antialiased}\n.slide{position:relative;width:1440px;height:810px;background:var(--bg);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;overflow:hidden;margin:0 auto 40px}\n.s-ov{position:absolute;inset:0;pointer-events:none;background:radial-gradient(120% 90% at 50% 0%,rgba(42,49,93,0.6) 0%,transparent 55%)}\n.brand{position:absolute;top:30px;right:44px;z-index:5;background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:12px;padding:8px 14px;font-weight:900;color:var(--text);font-size:18px;letter-spacing:.02em}\n.brand em{color:var(--accent);font-style:normal}\n.tag-v{position:absolute;top:34px;left:44px;z-index:5;font-size:12px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.14em;color:var(--dim)}\n.s-wrap{position:relative;z-index:2;width:100%;max-width:1280px;padding:0 72px;display:flex;flex-direction:column}\n.s-wrap.centered{align-items:center;text-align:center}\n.label-sm{font-size:11px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.16em;color:var(--accent);margin-bottom:20px}\n.rule{width:40px;height:2px;background:var(--accent);margin:0 auto 26px;opacity:.75;border-radius:2px}\n.h-xl{font-size:clamp(44px,6.4vw,84px);font-weight:900;line-height:1.03;letter-spacing:-.02em;color:var(--text)}\n.h-xl em{font-style:normal;color:var(--accent)}\n.body-t{font-size:18px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.8;color:var(--gray);max-width:680px}\n\n/* ---------- Variant A: horizontal roadmap ---------- */\n.road{display:flex;align-items:stretch;width:100%;max-width:1120px;margin-top:52px;position:relative}\n.road::before{content:'';position:absolute;top:34px;right:9%;left:9%;height:2px;background:linear-gradient(90deg,var(--border),var(--border-acc),var(--border));z-index:0}\n.rstep{flex:1;display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:center;text-align:center;position:relative;z-index:1;padding:0 10px}\n.rnode{width:68px;height:68px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--card);border:1.5px solid var(--border-acc);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-size:26px;font-weight:900;color:var(--accent);margin-bottom:20px;box-shadow:0 0 0 6px var(--bg)}\n.rstep.hot .rnode{background:var(--card-hi);box-shadow:0 0 0 6px var(--bg),0 0 22px rgba(47,194,188,.35)}\n.rstep h3{font-size:19px;font-weight:900;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:8px}\n.rstep p{font-size:13.5px;line-height:1.55;color:var(--gray);max-width:200px}\n.road-foot{margin-top:56px;max-width:760px;font-size:15px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--gray)}\n.road-foot b{color:var(--accent);font-weight:800}\n\n/* ---------- Variant B: editorial chapters ---------- */\n.ed-wrap{width:100%;max-width:1080px;display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:flex-start;text-align:right}\n.ed-lead{font-size:clamp(38px,4.6vw,60px);font-weight:900;line-height:1.12;letter-spacing:-.02em;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:14px}\n.ed-lead em{font-style:normal;color:var(--accent)}\n.ed-sub{font-size:17px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--gray);max-width:640px;margin-bottom:44px}\n.ed-chapters{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:14px 26px;width:100%}\n.ed-ch{display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:16px;background:var(--card);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:14px;padding:18px 20px}\n.ed-ch .num{flex:0 0 auto;width:40px;height:40px;border-radius:11px;background:var(--brand);border:1px solid var(--border-acc);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-size:20px;line-height:1}\n.ed-ch h4{font-size:17px;font-weight:900;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:3px}\n.ed-ch p{font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;color:var(--gray)}\n</style></head>\n<body>\n\n<!-- ============ VARIANT A ============ -->\n<section class=\"slide\" id=\"A\">\n  <div class=\"s-ov\"></div>\n  <div class=\"brand\">Pa<em>Y</em>Me</div>\n  <div class=\"tag-v\">גרסה A</div>\n  <div class=\"s-wrap centered\">\n    <div class=\"label-sm\">איך ניגשתי</div>\n    <div class=\"rule\"></div>\n    <h1 class=\"h-xl\">אסטרטגיה, <em>לא רשימה.</em></h1>\n    <div class=\"road\">\n      <div class=\"rstep hot\"><div class=\"rnode\">1</div><h3>אפיון שוק</h3><p>5 סגמנטים, 40+ שותפים פוטנציאליים על השולחן.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"rstep\"><div class=\"rnode\">2</div><h3>חלוקה</h3><p>Inbound מול Outbound — מינוף הקיים מול פתיחת חדש.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"rstep\"><div class=\"rnode\">3</div><h3>מתודה</h3><p>תעדוף שותפים לפי רמת בשלות, לא לפי כמות.</p></div>\n      <div class=\"rstep\"><div class=\"rnode\">4</div><h3>הכסף</h3><p>מהשותף הנכון עד ל-100,000 ₪ MRR נטו.</p></div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"road-foot\">בדקות הקרובות אני עובר על הפלואו הזה מקצה לקצה: <b>מאיפה התחלתי, איך חילקתי, לפי מה בחרתי, ואיך זה מגיע למספר.</b></div>\n  </div>\n</section>\n\n<!-- ============ VARIANT B ============ -->\n<section class=\"slide\" id=\"B\">\n  <div class=\"s-ov\"></div>\n  <div class=\"brand\">Pa<em>Y</em>Me</div>\n  <div class=\"tag-v\">גרסה B</div>\n  <div class=\"s-wrap\">\n    <div class=\"ed-wrap\">\n      <div class=\"label-sm\">איך ניגשתי</div>\n      <div class=\"rule\" style=\"margin-right:0;margin-left:auto;margin:0 0 22px 0\"></div>\n      <h1 class=\"ed-lead\">לא ניתחתי שוק.<br><em>בניתי מפה שאפשר לפתוח בה את היום הראשון.</em></h1>\n      <p class=\"ed-sub\">ארבעה חלקים שמרכיבים את התמונה. תכף אני לוקח אותך על כל אחד מהם.</p>\n      <div class=\"ed-chapters\">\n        <div class=\"ed-ch\"><div class=\"num\">🔍</div><div><h4>אפיון שוק</h4><p>מי בכלל השחקנים, ואיפה הפוטנציאל האמיתי.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"ed-ch\"><div class=\"num\">🔀</div><div><h4>חלוקה — Inbound / Outbound</h4><p>מינוף שותפים קיימים מול פתיחת קרקע חדשה.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"ed-ch\"><div class=\"num\">🎯</div><div><h4>מתודה — תעדוף שותפים</h4><p>לזהות את הנכון בזמן הנכון, לא לרדוף אחרי כמות.</p></div></div>\n        <div class=\"ed-ch\"><div class=\"num\">💰</div><div><h4>הכסף — הדרך ל-MRR</h4><p>איך כל זה מצטבר ל-100,000 ₪ נטו בחודש.</p></div></div>\n      </div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</section>\n\n</body></html>\n","numLines":92,"startLine":1,"totalLines":92}},"displayPath":"opening_mockups.html"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"37ca0d26-c6c8-446e-bb1a-ac3dbca4860c","timestamp":"2026-07-23T06:48:04.025Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/workspace/agent/payme_deck","sessionId":"9539cd90-0d50-404f-847a-2716aa5d1b61","version":"2.1.197","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"smooth-tickling-squid"}
{"parentUuid":"37ca0d26-c6c8-446e-bb1a-ac3dbca4860c","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"compact_file_reference","filename":"/workspace/agent/payme_deck/en.html","displayPath":"en.html"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"f80a621a-e17d-4ad8-9367-083b9155b211","timestamp":"2026-07-23T06:48:03.980Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/workspace/agent/payme_deck","sessionId":"9539cd90-0d50-404f-847a-2716aa5d1b61","version":"2.1.197","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"smooth-tickling-squid"}
{"parentUuid":"f80a621a-e17d-4ad8-9367-083b9155b211","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/workspace/agent/master_prompt_whatsapp_claude_code.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/agent/master_prompt_whatsapp_claude_code.md","content":"# MASTER PROMPT — Bring a NanoClaw agent onto WhatsApp (pilot-grade, privacy-isolated)\n\nPaste this whole block into Claude Code on the daniela VM (the machine that holds the provision/pilot code). Run it on the strongest available model. Work the LOOP below — do not free-style.\n\n---\n\n## MISSION\nMake a NanoClaw agent reachable and fully conversational over **WhatsApp**, at test/pilot grade, end to end: a real person messages a WhatsApp number, a NanoClaw agent answers, back and forth, reliably. This proves feasibility so we can show it works today and later scale it to pilot users.\n\n## THE ONE HARD CONSTRAINT (do not violate, ever)\n**The owner (Elia) must NEVER see the agent's WhatsApp conversations inside his own personal WhatsApp app.** This is non-negotiable and it dictates the architecture.\n\nWhy it dictates everything (already researched, treat as given):\n- WhatsApp \"linked device\" libraries (Baileys, whatsapp-web.js, Whapi) attach as a **companion device** to an account. A companion device **shares the same account and full message history** as the primary phone. So if you link the agent to Elia's personal number, **every agent chat appears in his WhatsApp app** and the device shows under Settings → Linked Devices. This FAILS the constraint.\n- Therefore **isolation is achieved by number choice, not by library choice.** The agent must run on a **dedicated number that Elia does not use in any WhatsApp app** — never his personal number.\n- The official **Cloud API** path makes a number API-only (removed from the WhatsApp app entirely) → isolated by construction, but needs Meta verification + webhook + templates (slower). That is the production path, not the pilot path.\n\n**Decision for THIS build (pilot):** Baileys linked to a **dedicated number** (a real prepaid SIM/eSIM from a carrier, or a paid single-use SMS-verification number for a throwaway pilot — NOT a VoIP number like Google Voice/TextNow, which WhatsApp rejects). Never Elia's personal number. If a dedicated number is not yet available when you start, build and test everything against a placeholder/second test number, and make the number a single config value so swapping it in is trivial.\n\n## RECOMMENDED STACK (unless Phase 0 finds something already in place)\n- Connection layer: **@whiskeysockets/baileys v7** (TypeScript, WebSocket, ~50 MB, no Chromium, sub-second start). It links via QR or 8-digit pairing code and mirrors NanoClaw's existing Telegram long-poll model (self-contained process, **no inbound webhook needed**). This is the closest fit to how NanoClaw already works.\n- Reference starters (read, don't copy blindly): fathonix/ChatGPT-WhatsApp (Baileys+LLM, TS), yesbhautik/Whatsapp-Ai-BOT, WhiskeySockets/Baileys examples.\n\n---\n\n## THE LOOP — how to work\nFor EVERY phase below:\n1. **Do** the smallest complete change for that phase.\n2. **Test** it with the concrete DONE-TEST written in the phase. Run a real command / send a real message — never assume.\n3. If the DONE-TEST fails: **diagnose the actual cause**, fix, and repeat step 2. Do not advance on a red test. Do not paper over with mocks.\n4. When green: **log** one line (what you did + the passing evidence) and advance.\nTop-level loop: keep iterating until ALL verification gates (V1–V6) are green AND a real human round-trip works. If you get stuck on the same gate twice, STOP and write a precise blocker note (what you tried, exact error, what you need) instead of thrashing.\n\nRules: change the minimum; reuse NanoClaw's existing patterns (mirror telegram.ts / telegram-pilot.ts, don't invent a parallel architecture); keep the WhatsApp number and all secrets in config/env, never hard-coded; never touch the ONECLI_BIND_HOST / gateway config; run ONE instance of any long-poll/socket process (double-launch breaks inbound — same rule as the Telegram pilot).\n\n---\n\n## PHASE 0 — INVESTIGATE FIRST (do not write code yet)\nNanoClaw already renders WhatsApp @-mentions and handles `@s.whatsapp.net` / `@g.us` JIDs, which strongly implies a WhatsApp adapter may ALREADY exist. Find out before building anything.\n- Search `src/channels/` and the whole repo for: `whatsapp`, `baileys`, `whatsapp-web`, `@s.whatsapp.net`, `WASocket`, `makeWASocket`.\n- Read how the Telegram channel is structured end to end: `src/channels/telegram.ts`, `src/channels/telegram-pilot.ts`, `src/provision-handler.ts`, and how a channel registers/initializes, how inbound messages become agent turns, how outbound is sent, and how sessions/auth state persist.\n- Determine the channel interface an adapter must implement (init, receive→dispatch, send, identity/JID handling, media).\n**DONE-TEST (V1):** produce a short written map: (a) does a WhatsApp adapter already exist? if yes, where and how complete; (b) the exact channel interface a new/updated adapter must satisfy; (c) the precise files you will add or change. If an adapter exists, the rest of this build is mostly WIRING + the dedicated-number privacy guarantee, not a from-scratch build — say so.\n\n## PHASE 1 — CONNECT (agent socket up on the dedicated number)\nImplement (or finish) a Baileys-based WhatsApp channel that: connects, persists auth/session state to disk so restarts don't require re-pairing, auto-reconnects on drop, and logs connection state clearly. Pair it to the **dedicated** number via QR/pairing code.\n**DONE-TEST (V2):** the process starts, pairs once, and logs a stable \"connected\" state; kill and restart the process → it reconnects WITHOUT re-pairing (session state persisted). Confirm exactly one socket instance is running.\n\n## PHASE 2 — RECEIVE → AGENT → REPLY (the round-trip)\nWire inbound WhatsApp messages into the NanoClaw agent turn pipeline (same path Telegram uses) and send the agent's reply back over WhatsApp. Map WhatsApp JID ↔ NanoClaw conversation/session correctly so context persists per contact.\n**DONE-TEST (V3):** from a second real phone, send a message to the dedicated number → a NanoClaw agent replies in WhatsApp within seconds → send a follow-up → the agent keeps context. Screenshot/log the thread.\n\n## PHASE 3 — PRIVACY PROOF (the constraint, verified)\nProve the isolation is real.\n**DONE-TEST (V4):** demonstrate that the agent is running on the dedicated number, NOT Elia's personal number, and that nothing appears in Elia's personal WhatsApp. Concretely: confirm the number in use ≠ Elia's number; confirm Elia's phone shows no new linked device and no new chats; document that swapping the number is one config change. Write this as an explicit pass/fail statement.\n\n## PHASE 4 — WIRE INTO PROVISION/PILOT (so it's not a one-off)\nConnect the WhatsApp channel to the existing provision/pilot flow the way `telegram-pilot` is wired, so a provisioned agent can be reached on WhatsApp (dedicated number per agent, or one number routing by contact for the pilot — pick the simplest that works and note the choice). Keep formatting correct (the existing WhatsApp @-mention/JID formatting rules).\n**DONE-TEST (V5):** a provisioned agent (not a hand-run script) answers over WhatsApp end to end, using the standard channel registration — no manual glue per message.\n\n## PHASE 5 — HARDEN FOR A SHORT PILOT\nHandle: reconnect after network drop, media/voice inbound at least gracefully (transcribe or acknowledge), long-message/formatting correctness, and ban-risk hygiene (warm the number slowly, only message opted-in testers, never bulk-blast — unofficial numbers get permanently banned on spam patterns). Back up the session/auth state.\n**DONE-TEST (V6):** run a 10-message mixed conversation (text + one media) across a reconnect; all handled without crash or lost context; auth state backed up and documented.\n\n---\n\n## DEFINITION OF DONE (pilot)\nAll of V1–V6 green, and: a real person on a normal phone holds a genuine back-and-forth with a NanoClaw agent over WhatsApp on a dedicated number, with zero visibility of those chats in Elia's personal WhatsApp. \n\n## FINAL REPORT (write this back)\n1. What existed vs. what you built (Phase 0 finding first).\n2. Exact files added/changed.\n3. The dedicated-number setup: what number type, how paired, where session state lives, how to swap the number.\n4. How to start/stop it safely (single-instance rule).\n5. The privacy proof statement (V4).\n6. Known limits + the honest ban-risk note, and the concrete next step to production (Cloud API via 360dialog: dedicated API-only number, webhook on the existing provision tunnel, pre-approved templates, per-message pricing).\n7. Anything you were blocked on and exactly what you need from Elia (most likely: the dedicated SIM/eSIM number to pair).\n\n## KEY FACTS (so you don't re-derive them)\n- Personal-number link = owner sees all chats → FORBIDDEN. Dedicated number only.\n- VoIP numbers (Google Voice, TextNow) are rejected by WhatsApp verification; data-only travel eSIMs (Airalo) can't receive the verification code. Use a real carrier prepaid SIM/eSIM, or a paid single-use mobile SMS-verification number for a throwaway pilot.\n- Baileys is unofficial (ban risk) — fine for a pilot on a number that doesn't matter; never on a number we care about. Production = official Cloud API.\n- Mirror the Telegram channel's architecture; one running instance only; secrets in env; don't touch the OneCLI gateway bind host.\n","numLines":84,"startLine":1,"totalLines":84}},"displayPath":"../master_prompt_whatsapp_claude_code.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"fa9bdc73-7453-41b2-84d7-aa3e52b102ef","timestamp":"2026-07-23T06:48:03.969Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-ts","cwd":"/workspace/agent/payme_deck","sessionId":"9539cd90-0d50-404f-847a-2716aa5d1b61","version":"2.1.197","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"smooth-tickling-squid"}
