h, not the pilot path.\n17\t\n18\t**Decision for THIS build (pilot):** Baileys linked to a **dedicated number** (a real prepaid SIM/eSIM from a carrier, or a paid single-use SMS-verification number for a throwaway pilot — NOT a VoIP number like Google Voice/TextNow, which WhatsApp rejects). Never Elia's personal number. If a dedicated number is not yet available when you start, build and test everything against a placeholder/second test number, and make the number a single config value so swapping it in is trivial.\n19\t\n20\t## RECOMMENDED STACK (unless Phase 0 finds something already in place)\n21\t- Connection layer: **@whiskeysockets/baileys v7** (TypeScript, WebSocket, ~50 MB, no Chromium, sub-second start). It links via QR or 8-digit pairing code and mirrors NanoClaw's existing Telegram long-poll model (self-contained process, **no inbound webhook needed**). This is the closest fit to how NanoClaw already works.\n22\t- Reference starters (read, don't copy blindly): fathonix/ChatGPT-WhatsApp (Baileys+LLM, TS), yesbhautik/Whatsapp-Ai-BOT, WhiskeySockets/Baileys examples.\n23\t\n24\t---\n25\t\n26\t## THE LOOP — how to work\n27\tFor EVERY phase below:\n28\t1. **Do** the smallest complete change for that phase.\n29\t2. **Test** it with the concrete DONE-TEST written in the phase. Run a real command / send a real message — never assume.\n30\t3. If the DONE-TEST fails: **diagnose the actual cause**, fix, and repeat step 2. Do not advance on a red test. Do not paper over with mocks.\n31\t4. When green: **log** one line (what you did + the passing evidence) and advance.\n32\tTop-level loop: keep iterating until ALL verification gates (V1–V6) are green AND a real human round-trip works. If you get stuck on the same gate twice, STOP and write a precise blocker note (what you tried, exact error, what you need) instead of thrashing.\n33\t\n34\tRules: change the minimum; reuse NanoClaw's existing patterns (mirror telegram.ts / telegram-pilot.ts, don't invent a parallel architecture); keep the WhatsApp number and all secrets in config/env, never hard-coded; never touch the ONECLI_BIND_HOST / gateway config; run ONE instance of any long-poll/socket process (double-launch breaks inbound — same rule as the Telegram pilot).\n35\t\n36\t---\n37\t\n38\t## PHASE 0 — INVESTIGATE FIRST (do not write code yet)\n39\tNanoClaw already renders WhatsApp @-mentions and handles `@s.whatsapp.net` / `@g.us` JIDs, which strongly implies a WhatsApp adapter may ALREADY exist. Find out before building anything.\n40\t- Search `src/channels/` and the whole repo for: `whatsapp`, `baileys`, `whatsapp-web`, `@s.whatsapp.net`, `WASocket`, `makeWASocket`.\n41\t- Read how the Telegram channel is structured end to end: `src/channels/telegram.ts`, `src/channels/telegram-pilot.ts`, `src/provision-handler.ts`, and how a channel registers/initializes, how inbound messages become agent turns, how outbound is sent, and how sessions/auth state persist.\n42\t- Determine the channel interface an adapter must implement (init, receive→dispatch, send, identity/JID handling, media).\n43\t**DONE-TEST (V1):** produce a short written map: (a) does a WhatsApp adapter already exist? if yes, where and how complete; (b) the exact channel interface a new/updated adapter must satisfy; (c) the precise files you will add or change. If an adapter exists, the rest of this build is mostly WIRING + the dedicated-number privacy guarantee, not a from-scratch build — say so.\n44\t\n45\t## PHASE 1 — CONNECT (agent socket up on the dedicated number)\n46\tImplement (or finish) a Baileys-based WhatsApp channel that: connects, persists auth/session state to disk so restarts don't require re-pairing, auto-reconnects on drop, and logs connection state clearly. Pair it to the **dedicated** number via QR/pairing code.\n47\t**DONE-TEST (V2):** the process starts, pairs once, and logs a stable \"connected\" state; kill and restart the process → it reconnects WITHOUT re-pairing (session state persisted). Confirm exactly one socket instance is running.\n48\t\n49\t## PHASE 2 — RECEIVE → AGENT → REPLY (the round-trip)\n50\tWire inbound WhatsApp messages into the NanoClaw agent turn pipeline (same path Telegram uses) and send the agent's reply back over WhatsApp. Map WhatsApp JID ↔ NanoClaw conversation/session correctly so context persists per contact.\n51\t**DONE-TEST (V3):** from a second real phone, send a message to the dedicated number → a NanoClaw agent replies in WhatsApp within seconds → send a follow-up → the agent keeps context. Screenshot/log the thread.\n52\t\n53\t## PHASE 3 — PRIVACY PROOF (the constraint, verified)\n54\tProve the isolation is real.\n55\t**DONE-TEST (V4):** demonstrate that the agent is running on the dedicated number, NOT Elia's personal number, and that nothing appears in Elia's personal WhatsApp. Concretely: confirm the number in use ≠ Elia's number; confirm Elia's phone shows no new linked device and no new chats; document that swapping the number is one config change. Write this as an explicit pass/fail statement.\n56\t\n57\t## PHASE 4 — WIRE INTO PROVISION/PILOT (so it's not a one-off)\n58\tConnect the WhatsApp channel to the existing provision/pilot flow the way `telegram-pilot` is wired, so a provisioned agent can be reached on WhatsApp (dedicated number per agent, or one number routing by contact for the pilot — pick the simplest that works and note the choice). Keep formatting correct (the existing WhatsApp @-mention/JID formatting rules).\n59\t**DONE-TEST (V5):** a provisioned agent (not a hand-run script) answers over WhatsApp end to end, using the standard channel registration — no manual glue per message.\n60\t\n61\t## PHASE 5 — HARDEN FOR A SHORT PILOT\n62\tHandle: reconnect after network drop, media/voice inbound at least gracefully (transcribe or acknowledge), long-message/formatting correctness, and ban-risk hygiene (warm the number slowly, only message opted-in testers, never bulk-blast — unofficial numbers get permanently banned on spam patterns). Back up the session/auth state.\n63\t**DONE-TEST (V6):** run a 10-message mixed conversation (text + one media) across a reconnect; all handled without crash or lost context; auth state backed up and documented.\n64\t\n65\t---\n66\t\n67\t## DEFINITION OF DONE (pilot)\n68\tAll of V1–V6 green, and: a real person on a normal phone holds a genuine back-and-forth with a NanoClaw agent over WhatsApp on a dedicated number, with zero visibility of those chats in Elia's personal WhatsApp. \n69\t\n70\t## FINAL REPORT (write this back)\n71\t1. What existed vs. what you built (Phase 0 finding first).\n72\t2. Exact files added/changed.\n73\t3. The dedicated-number setup: what number type, how paired, where session state lives, how to swap the number.\n74\t4. How to start/stop it safely (single-instance rule).\n75\t5. The privacy proof statement (V4).\n76\t6. Known limits + the honest ban-risk note, and the concrete next step to production (Cloud API via 360dialog: dedicated API-only number, webhook on the existing provision tunnel, pre-approved templates, per-message pricing).\n77\t7. Anything you were blocked on and exactly what you need from Elia (most likely: the dedicated SIM/eSIM number to pair).\n78\t\n79\t## KEY FACTS (so you don't re-derive them)\n80\t- Personal-number link = owner sees all chats → FORBIDDEN. Dedicated number only.\n81\t- VoIP numbers (Google Voice, TextNow) are rejected by WhatsApp verification; data-only travel eSIMs (Airalo) can't receive the verification code. Use a real carrier prepaid SIM/eSIM, or a paid single-use mobile SMS-verification number for a throwaway pilot.\n82\t- Baileys is unofficial (ban risk) — fine for a pilot on a number that doesn't matter; never on a number we care about. Production = official Cloud API.\n83\t- Mirror the Telegram channel's architecture; one running instance only; secrets in env; don't touch the OneCLI gateway bind host.\n84\t"}]},"uuid":"0c393820-542e-4f0e-9f34-fe1440e34b81","timestamp":"