edicated number, with zero visibility of those chats inside Elia's personal WhatsApp app.</p>\n\n<h2>&lt;hard_constraint&gt;</h2>\n<p><b>The owner (Elia) must NEVER see the agent's WhatsApp conversations inside his own personal WhatsApp app.</b> This is non-negotiable and it dictates the architecture. Treat any design that risks violating it as wrong by definition.</p>\n<p>Why it dictates everything (already researched — treat as given, do not re-litigate):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>WhatsApp \"linked device\" libraries (Baileys, whatsapp-web.js, Whapi) attach as a <b>companion device</b> to an account. A companion device <b>shares the same account and full message history</b> as the primary phone. Linking the agent to Elia's personal number would surface every agent chat in his WhatsApp app and list the device under Settings → Linked Devices. That FAILS the constraint.</li>\n<li>Therefore <b>isolation is achieved by number choice, not by library choice.</b> The agent must run on a <b>dedicated number that Elia does not use in any WhatsApp app</b> — never his personal number.</li>\n<li>The official <b>Cloud API</b> path makes a number API-only (removed from the WhatsApp app entirely) → isolated by construction, but needs Meta verification, a webhook, and message templates (slower). That is the production path, not this pilot.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Decision for this build (pilot):</b> Baileys linked to a <b>dedicated number</b> — a real prepaid SIM / eSIM from a carrier, or a paid single-use SMS-verification number for a throwaway pilot. A VoIP number (Google Voice, TextNow) is rejected by WhatsApp; a data-only travel eSIM (Airalo) cannot receive the verification code. Never Elia's personal number. If a dedicated number is not yet available when you start, build and test against a placeholder / second test number and keep the number as a single config value so swapping it in later is one edit.</p>\n\n<h2>&lt;recommended_stack&gt;</h2>\n<p>Use this unless Phase 0 finds something already in place:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Connection layer: <b>@whiskeysockets/baileys v7</b> (TypeScript, WebSocket, ~50 MB, no Chromium, sub-second start). It links via QR or 8-digit pairing code and mirrors NanoClaw's existing Telegram long-poll model: a self-contained process, <b>no inbound webhook required</b>. This is the closest fit to how NanoClaw already works.</li>\n<li>Reference starters (read for structure, do not copy blindly): fathonix/ChatGPT-WhatsApp (Baileys + LLM, TS), yesbhautik/Whatsapp-Ai-BOT, WhiskeySockets/Baileys examples.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>&lt;workflow&gt;</h2>\n<p>Work in a tight loop. For EVERY phase below:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Plan.</b> Before editing, state in one short paragraph what you will change and why. For Phase 0, plan your investigation.</li>\n<li><b>Do</b> the smallest complete change that satisfies the phase.</li>\n<li><b>Test</b> against the phase's concrete DONE-TEST. Run a real command or send a real message — never assume. Capture the evidence (log line, screenshot, command output).</li>\n<li><b>If red:</b> diagnose the actual root cause, fix, and re-test. Do not advance on a failing test. Do not paper over with mocks or stubbed responses.</li>\n<li><b>When green:</b> log one line — what you did plus the passing evidence — and advance.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Top-level loop:</b> keep iterating until all verification gates V1–V6 are green AND a real human round-trip works. If you hit the same gate twice without progress, STOP and write a precise blocker note (what you tried, the exact error, what you need) rather than thrashing.</p>\n<p><b>Standing rules:</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Explore before you change. Read the real code; do not assume how NanoClaw is structured. Use a sub-agent for the Phase 0 investigation if it speeds a broad read.</li>\n<li>Change the minimum. Reuse NanoClaw's existing patterns — mirror <code>telegram.ts</code> / <code>telegram-pilot.ts</code>; do not invent a parallel architecture.</li>\n<li>Keep the WhatsApp number and all secrets in config / env, never hard-coded.</li>\n<li>Never touch the <code>ONECLI_BIND_HOST</code> or the OneCLI gateway config.</li>\n<li>Run exactly ONE instance of any long-poll / socket process. Double-launch breaks inbound — the same failure mode already documented for the Telegram pilot.</li>\n<li>Follow the surrounding code's style and conventions; match it rather than reformatting.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>&lt;phases&gt;</h2>\n\n<h3>Phase 0 — Investigate first (write no code yet)</h3>\n<p>NanoClaw already renders WhatsApp @-mentions and handles <code>@s.whatsapp.net</code> / <code>@g.us</code> JIDs, which strongly implies a WhatsApp adapter may ALREADY exist. Find out before building anything.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Search <code>src/channels/</code> and the whole repo for: <code>whatsapp</code>, <code>baileys</code>, <code>whatsapp-web</code>, <code>@s.whatsapp.net</code>, <code>WASocket</code>, <code>makeWASocket</code>.</li>\n<li>Read the Telegram channel end to end: <code>src/channels/telegram.ts</code>, <code>src/channels/telegram-pilot.ts</code>, <code>src/provision-handler.ts</code>. Understand how a channel registers and initializes, how inbound messages become agent turns, how outbound is sent, and how session / auth state persists.</li>\n<li>Determine the exact channel interface an adapter must implement (init, receive→dispatch, send, identity / JID handling, media).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>DONE-TEST (V1):</b> produce a short written map — (a) does a WhatsApp adapter already exist, and if so where and how complete; (b) the exact channel interface a new or updated adapter must satisfy; (c) the precise files you will add or change. If an adapter already exists, say so plainly: the rest of this build is then mostly WIRING plus the dedicated-number privacy guarantee, not a from-scratch build.</p>\n\n<h3>Phase 1 — Connect (agent socket up on the dedicated number)</h3>\n<p>Implement or finish a Baileys-based WhatsApp channel that connects, persists auth / session state to